CC(1)                          P32W Unix                           CC(1)

NAME
       cc - C compiler

SYNOPSIS
       cc [-c | -S | -E] [-o out] [-I dir] [-D name[=val]] [-lname]
          [-s] [-v] [-w] file ...
       cc -h

DESCRIPTION
       cc compiles C programs for P32W Unix. Each argument is treated
       by its suffix:

       .c     preprocessed and compiled to assembly language, which
              is then assembled by /bin/as into an object
       .s     assembled by /bin/as
       other  (.o objects, .a archives) passed to the linker as is

       Unless -c, -S or -E is given, cc then runs /bin/ld to link

              /usr/lib/crt0.o  the objects, in argument order
              /usr/lib/libc.a

       into an executable a.out (or the file named by -o). crt0.o
       defines the entry point _start, which calls
       main(argc, argv, envp) and then exit with main's result.

       The preprocessor is built into the compiler; there is no
       separate cpp. Each .c file is compiled in a process of its
       own, so every file has the whole address space to work in.
       Intermediate files are /tmp/ccPID_N.s and /tmp/ccPID_N.o
       (PID the process id of cc, N the file's place among the
       files named, from 0); they are removed when cc is done with
       them.

       cc stops at the first file that fails to compile or
       assemble. The compiler itself stops at the first error, which
       it reports as file:line: error: message. Warnings have the
       form file:line: warning: message and do not stop it.

OPTIONS
       -c     compile or assemble only: leave file.o in the current
              directory for each .c or .s file (named after the
              source, without its directory). Other files are
              ignored.
       -S     compile only: leave file.s in the current directory for
              each .c file, to read the code the compiler made: each
              statement's source line is a comment above its code
              (; prog.c:12: n = n + 1;). Other files are ignored. With
              -o - the assembly goes to the screen: cc -S -o - prog.c |
              more.
       -E     preprocess the first file only and write the result to
              the standard output (or to out, with -o; -o - is the
              standard output too).
       -o out
              name the output: the executable, or with -E the
              preprocessed text. With -c or -S it names the object or
              assembly file only when there is a single input file;
              otherwise it is ignored.
       -I dir, -Idir
              add dir to the directories searched for #include files.
       -D name, -Dname
              define name as 1.
       -D name=val
              define name as val; -Dname= defines it as nothing.
       -lname link the library /usr/lib/libname.a (put it after the
              files that use it).
       -s     leave the symbol table out of the program (ld -s): a
              smaller file, but db(1) then shows addresses, not names.
       -v     show the as and ld commands as they run.
       -w     suppress warnings.
       -h     list the options (also shown for an unknown option).

       #include "file" is searched for first in the directory of the
       file containing the #include, then in the -I directories in
       the order given, then in /usr/include. #include <file> skips
       the first step.

LANGUAGE
       The compiler accepts C89 (ANSI C) and the common parts of
       C99. Its types are char (8 bits, signed), short (16), int and
       long (32), long long (64), their unsigned forms, double (64,
       IEEE; float and long double are the same type), pointers
       (32), arrays, struct, union, enum, typedef and pointers to
       functions. Alignment is the size, up to 4. _Bool is taken
       as unsigned char. const, volatile, inline, auto and register
       are accepted; register is only a hint (the compiler keeps
       busy locals in r10-r13 by itself).

       Supported: initialisers (nested, partial, strings, address
       constants, designated: [i] = and .m =, with brace elision),
       compound literals, __func__, bit-fields, structures, long
       longs and doubles passed and returned by value, switch, goto,
       variadic
       functions through <stdarg.h>, // comments, adjacent string
       concatenation, octal, hex and \x escapes (also \e), integer
       suffixes u, l and ul, C89 implicit int, and calls to
       undeclared functions (taken as returning int, with a
       warning).

       The preprocessor handles #include (also with a macro naming
       the file), #define with # and ## and variadic macros
       (__VA_ARGS__), #undef, #if, #ifdef, #ifndef, #elif, #else,
       #endif (nested up to 64 deep), defined, #error and
       #pragma once; #line, #ident and other #pragma lines are
       ignored. Predefined macros: __P32__ and __STDC__ (both 1),
       __FILE__ and __LINE__.

       long long and double live in memory and their operations are
       library calls (libc's llong.c, dbl.c on softfp(3)): many
       times slower than int, but exact (+ - * / are correctly
       rounded, as on any IEEE machine). A function with a
       structure, long long or double parameter takes all its
       arguments on the stack; one returning such a value gets a
       hidden first argument where to put it (D30).

       Not supported, each a compile-time error: old-style (K&R)
       function definitions and hexadecimal floating constants.
       Calling printf, fprintf, sprintf, snprintf, scanf, fscanf,
       sscanf, open, execl, execlp or execle without its header is
       an error, not a warning, because variadic functions are
       called differently.

       Warnings are given for implicit declarations, incompatible
       pointer types, conversions between integers and pointers,
       comparison of a pointer with an integer, and excess array
       initialisers.

CALLING CONVENTION
       Arguments 1-4 are passed in r1-r4, the rest on the stack (the
       fifth at the lowest address); the result is returned in r1.
       r1-r7 are caller-saved, r8-r13 callee-saved, r14 is the frame
       pointer and r15 the stack pointer; CALL pushes the return
       address. A variadic function receives all its arguments on
       the stack, 4 bytes each, so a va_list is a plain pointer.
       Arguments are evaluated left to right. A C name that is also
       a register name (r1, sp, ...) becomes _C_name in assembly.

EXAMPLES
       cc -o hello hello.c           compile and link a program
       cc -c util.c                  make util.o
       cc -o prog main.c util.o      link with an existing object
       cc -S x.c                     look at the assembly in x.s
       cc -E -DDEBUG x.c | more      see what the preprocessor made

FILES
       /bin/as               the assembler
       /bin/ld               the linker
       /usr/include          standard headers
       /usr/lib/crt0.o       program start-up
       /usr/lib/libc.a       the C library
       /tmp/ccPID_N.[so]     temporary files
       a.out                 default output

ENVIRONMENT
       CCMEM  if set, the compiler reports on the standard error how
              many bytes of heap each .c file needed.

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if preprocessing, compiling, assembling or
       linking failed; 2 for a usage error or an unknown option.

SEE ALSO
       as(1), ld(1), ar(1), db(1), a.out(5)

BUGS
       Each .c file is compiled in a process of its own, which
       holds the file's tokens (the source and its headers,
       preprocessed: 28 bytes each) and the largest function's tree
       at once. Lua's lvm.c, 53000 tokens with one function of
       35000, takes 3 MB; a process has 8 MiB of address space,
       shared RAM permitting. Split bigger programs into several
       files.

       float is double (8 bytes), so a float is more precise than
       on other machines and sizeof(float) is 8. There are no -O,
       -g, -L, -U or -M
       options. There is no optimiser beyond keeping locals in
       registers.

       long is 32 bits.

       If a later file fails, temporary objects of earlier files are
       left in /tmp. With -E only the first file is processed; with
       -S, .s and .o arguments are silently ignored, as are .o and .a
       arguments with -c.
